==========================================
SYSADMIN DAY 05 NOTES — iptables Part 2
Date: 19-10-2026
==========================================

WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- LOG records a packet but does NOT block it - the DROP/ACCEPT after it is a separate decision
- The nat table is completely separate from filter; MASQUERADE rewrites a packet's source address
  for routed traffic, used in POSTROUTING (after the routing decision is made)
- iptables rules are LOST on reboot unless saved; iptables-save/restore is the manual way,
  iptables-persistent is the automatic (every-boot) way
- -P sets a chain's DEFAULT policy (what happens when no rule matches)
- A safe base firewall allows loopback and ESTABLISHED,RELATED traffic FIRST, so replies to
  connections you started are never accidentally blocked by rules that come after
- Always know the undo (-F, then -P ... ACCEPT on every chain) before applying a default-DROP policy

COMMANDS / ACTIONS I PRACTICED:
[ ] iptables -A INPUT -p icmp -j LOG --log-prefix ... then -j DROP, checked journalctl/dmesg
[ ] iptables -t nat -L -v -n  /  -A POSTROUTING -j MASQUERADE  (read or applied)
[ ] iptables-save > file  /  iptables -F  /  iptables-restore < file
[ ] the base firewall script (Part D) - default DROP + loopback + established + SSH

